<!-- LESSON PLAN -->
<section id="lesson" class="section active">

  <div class="hero-card">
    <h2>Lesson Plan</h2>
    <p>A five-session learner-centered sequence for introducing, distinguishing, illustrating, and applying simple, general, and deferred annuities.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Lesson Information</h2>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="info"><strong>Learning Area</strong>General Mathematics</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Grade Level</strong>Grade 11</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Section</strong>To be completed by teacher</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Teacher</strong>To be completed by teacher</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Number of Sessions</strong>5</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Term</strong>Third Term</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Week</strong>2</div>
      <div class="info"><strong>Domain</strong>Number and Algebra</div>
    </div>

    <h3>Budget of Work Competency</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      Illustrate simple annuity, general annuity, and deferred annuity.
      Solve problems involving annuities.
    </div>

    <h3>Content Standard</h3>
    <p>The learners should have knowledge and understanding of compound interest; simple and general annuities; and business and consumer loans.</p>

    <h3>Performance Standard Connection</h3>
    <p>Learners develop financial understanding that can support a digital financial proposal comparing investments with data, graphs, and evidence-based recommendations.</p>

    <h3>References</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Grade 11 General Mathematics Three-Term Budget of Work, last updated April 17, 2026.</li>
      <li>DepEd General Mathematics curriculum resources and teacher-developed instructional materials.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Declaration of AI Use</h3>
    <p>This learning package was developed with AI-assisted instructional design. The teacher remains responsible for reviewing, contextualizing, validating, and approving the final material before classroom use.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Intentions</h2>

    <h3>Learning Objectives</h3>
    <p>By the end of the five-session sequence, learners should be able to:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Define and distinguish simple annuity, general annuity, ordinary annuity, and deferred annuity using appropriate mathematical terminology.</li>
      <li>Illustrate the payment and interest-conversion intervals of simple, general, and deferred annuities using timelines and practical examples.</li>
      <li>Identify the appropriate annuity type from Philippine financial situations such as savings, installment payments, and retirement planning.</li>
      <li>Solve selected annuity problems using appropriate formulas, substitutions, technology, and clear interpretation.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Learner Context and Planning Notes</h3>
    <p>The package is designed for average-level Grade 11 learners. Instruction begins with familiar payment situations before moving toward mathematical notation and computation. Teacher questioning should emphasize the relationship between payment frequency, interest-conversion frequency, and the starting time of payments.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Five-Session Instructional Flow</h2>

    <div class="stage">
      <span class="tag">SESSION 1 • CONNECT + EXPLORE</span>
      <h3>From Everyday Payments to Annuities</h3>
      <p><strong>Connect:</strong> Ask learners where they encounter repeated payments: savings deposits, tuition installments, appliance installments, insurance premiums, or retirement contributions.</p>
      <p><strong>Explore:</strong> Present three timelines with equal payments. Learners identify what stays the same and what changes.</p>
      <p><strong>Explain:</strong> Introduce the meaning of annuity as a sequence of equal payments made at equal time intervals.</p>
      <p><strong>Guided Practice:</strong> Learners classify familiar examples as repeated payment situations.</p>
      <p><strong>Formative Check:</strong> Ask: “What two intervals should we compare when identifying an annuity?”</p>
      <span class="badge">Contextualization</span>
      <span class="badge">Activating Prior Knowledge</span>
      <span class="badge">Formative Check</span>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <span class="tag">SESSION 2 • EXPLAIN + MODEL</span>
      <h3>Simple Annuity</h3>
      <p><strong>Review:</strong> Revisit equal payments and equal intervals.</p>
      <p><strong>Explain:</strong> A simple annuity has a payment interval that coincides with the interest-conversion interval.</p>
      <p><strong>Model:</strong> For ₱3,000 deposited monthly at a 6% nominal annual rate compounded monthly, identify i = 0.06 ÷ 12 = 0.005 and n = 24 for two years.</p>
      <p><strong>Guided Practice:</strong> Learners identify i and n in progressively varied situations.</p>
      <p><strong>Independent Practice:</strong> Learners solve selected future-value examples.</p>
      <span class="badge">Explicit Modeling</span>
      <span class="badge">Scaffolding</span>
      <span class="badge">Guided Practice</span>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <span class="tag">SESSION 3 • EXPLORE + COMPARE</span>
      <h3>General Annuity</h3>
      <p><strong>Explore:</strong> Compare monthly payments with monthly compounding against quarterly payments with monthly compounding.</p>
      <p><strong>Explain:</strong> A general annuity occurs when the payment interval differs from the interest-conversion interval.</p>
      <p><strong>Collaborative Learning:</strong> Groups classify financial situations and justify their answers.</p>
      <p><strong>Formative Check:</strong> Learners complete a “same interval or different interval?” classification grid.</p>
      <span class="badge">Compare and Contrast</span>
      <span class="badge">Collaborative Learning</span>
      <span class="badge">Reasoning</span>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <span class="tag">SESSION 4 • EXPLAIN + APPLY</span>
      <h3>Deferred Annuity</h3>
      <p><strong>Connect:</strong> Discuss situations where payments or benefits start later rather than immediately.</p>
      <p><strong>Explain:</strong> A deferred annuity begins payments after a stated waiting period.</p>
      <p><strong>Illustrate:</strong> Use a timeline to mark the waiting period, first payment, and succeeding payments.</p>
      <p><strong>Apply:</strong> Learners distinguish ordinary, general, and deferred situations.</p>
      <span class="badge">Visual Modeling</span>
      <span class="badge">Real-World Application</span>
      <span class="badge">Differentiation</span>
    </div>

    <div class="stage">
      <span class="tag">SESSION 5 • APPLY + REFLECT + MASTER</span>
      <h3>Integrated Problem Solving</h3>
      <p><strong>Review:</strong> Complete a classification matrix covering all annuity types.</p>
      <p><strong>Independent Practice:</strong> Learners solve computation and interpretation tasks.</p>
      <p><strong>Apply:</strong> Present a short Philippine financial scenario and ask learners to identify the annuity type and explain why.</p>
      <p><strong>Reflect:</strong> Learners complete the sentence: “The most important clue in identifying an annuity is...”</p>
      <p><strong>Mastery Check:</strong> Short assessment covering definitions, classification, and computations.</p>
      <span class="badge">Independent Practice</span>
      <span class="badge">Reflection</span>
      <span class="badge">Mastery Check</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Suggested Readiness Routine</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Recall percentage and decimal conversion.</li>
      <li>Recall that nominal annual rates must be converted according to the compounding frequency when appropriate.</li>
      <li>Review multiplication, division, exponents, and calculator use.</li>
      <li>Interpret a basic timeline showing months, quarters, and years.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

</section>

<!-- FACT SHEET -->
<section id="fact" class="section">

  <div class="hero-card">
    <h2>Student Study Notes</h2>
    <p>Week 2: Simple Annuity, General Annuity, and Deferred Annuity</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>What You Will Learn</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>What an annuity is.</li>
      <li>How to distinguish simple, general, and deferred annuities.</li>
      <li>How payment intervals and interest-conversion intervals are related.</li>
      <li>How to identify the number of payment periods.</li>
      <li>How to calculate selected future values of simple annuities.</li>
      <li>How annuities appear in everyday Philippine financial situations.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Key Ideas</h2>

    <div class="formula">
      An annuity is a sequence of equal payments made at equal time intervals.
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Type</th>
          <th>Key Identification</th>
          <th>Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Simple Annuity</td>
          <td>Payment interval and interest-conversion interval are the same.</td>
          <td>Monthly payments with monthly compounding.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>General Annuity</td>
          <td>Payment interval and interest-conversion interval are different.</td>
          <td>Quarterly payments with monthly compounding.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Deferred Annuity</td>
          <td>Payments begin after a waiting period.</td>
          <td>Benefits begin several months after the contract starts.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Ordinary Annuity</td>
          <td>Payments are made at the end of each period.</td>
          <td>Monthly payment made at the end of each month.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Essential Definitions</h2>

    <h3>Payment Interval</h3>
    <p>The amount of time between consecutive payments.</p>

    <h3>Interest-Conversion Interval</h3>
    <p>The period used to apply interest to the account.</p>

    <h3>Nominal Annual Rate</h3>
    <p>An annual stated interest rate that is divided according to the number of conversion periods per year when determining the periodic rate.</p>

    <h3>Number of Periods</h3>
    <p>The total number of payment periods during the stated time.</p>

    <h3>Future Value</h3>
    <p>The accumulated value of a sequence of payments at a specified future time.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Future Value of an Ordinary Simple Annuity</h2>

    <div class="formula">
      FV = R[(1 + i)ⁿ − 1] ÷ i
    </div>

    <p>Where:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>FV = future value</li>
      <li>R = regular payment</li>
      <li>i = interest rate per payment period</li>
      <li>n = number of payment periods</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning">
      Important: The formula above is directly applicable when the payment interval and interest-conversion interval coincide.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Step-by-Step Worked Examples</h2>

    <div class="example">
      <h3>Example 1: Identifying a Simple Annuity</h3>
      <p>A learner deposits ₱3,000 every month. The account earns 6% nominal annual interest compounded monthly.</p>

      <div class="step"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the payment interval: monthly.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the interest-conversion interval: monthly.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Step 3:</strong> Compare them.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The intervals match, so this is a simple annuity.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <h3>Example 2: Future Value of a Simple Annuity</h3>
      <p>A learner deposits ₱3,000 monthly for 2 years at 6% nominal annual interest compounded monthly. Find the accumulated value.</p>

      <div class="step"><strong>Step 1: Periodic rate</strong><br>
      i = 0.06 ÷ 12 = 0.005</div>

      <div class="step"><strong>Step 2: Number of periods</strong><br>
      n = 2 × 12 = 24</div>

      <div class="step"><strong>Step 3: Formula</strong><br>
      FV = R[(1 + i)ⁿ − 1] ÷ i</div>

      <div class="step"><strong>Step 4: Substitute</strong><br>
      FV = ₱3,000[(1.005)²⁴ − 1] ÷ 0.005</div>

      <div class="step"><strong>Step 5: Compute</strong><br>
      FV ≈ ₱76,295.87</div>

      <div class="success"><strong>Answer:</strong> The accumulated value is approximately ₱76,295.87.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <h3>Example 3: Identifying a General Annuity</h3>
      <p>A person pays ₱5,000 every quarter while the account is compounded monthly at a nominal annual rate.</p>
      <div class="step"><strong>Payment interval:</strong> quarterly.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Interest-conversion interval:</strong> monthly.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Conclusion:</strong> The intervals differ, so this is a general annuity.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="example">
      <h3>Example 4: Identifying a Deferred Annuity</h3>
      <p>A financial plan starts today, but the regular payments begin after a six-month waiting period.</p>
      <div class="step"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the first payment date.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Step 2:</strong> Identify the waiting period.</div>
      <div class="step"><strong>Step 3:</strong> Because the payments begin later, the annuity is deferred.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Common Mistakes and Pitfalls</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Confusing payment frequency with interest-conversion frequency.</li>
      <li>Using 6% directly as the monthly rate when the stated rate is 6% nominal compounded monthly.</li>
      <li>Forgetting to convert years into the number of payment periods.</li>
      <li>Calling every repeated-payment situation a simple annuity without checking the intervals.</li>
      <li>Ignoring the waiting period when identifying a deferred annuity.</li>
      <li>Rounding too early during a calculation.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Real-World Connections</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Regular savings deposits.</li>
      <li>Insurance premiums.</li>
      <li>Installment payments.</li>
      <li>Retirement income planning.</li>
      <li>Education savings.</li>
      <li>Investment contributions.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Quick Self-Check</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>If payments are monthly and interest is compounded monthly, what type of annuity is suggested?</li>
      <li>If payments are quarterly but interest is compounded monthly, what type is suggested?</li>
      <li>If payments begin only after a waiting period, what type is suggested?</li>
      <li>What should you compare before classifying a simple or general annuity?</li>
      <li>What does n represent in the future-value formula?</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

</section>

