<section id="notes" class="section">

  <div class="hero-card">
    <h2>Teacher Notes</h2>
    <p>Implementation guidance, misconceptions, remediation, enrichment, and contextualization.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Teaching Advice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Begin with actual payment schedules before introducing formulas.</li>
      <li>Always ask learners to identify the payment interval first.</li>
      <li>Then ask them to identify the interest-conversion interval.</li>
      <li>Use timelines to make deferred annuities visually clear.</li>
      <li>Require learners to state why an annuity belongs to a category instead of accepting labels without justification.</li>
      <li>Use Philippine peso amounts and familiar financial contexts.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Common Learner Misconceptions</h2>

    <table>
      <tr>
        <th>Misconception</th>
        <th>Correction Strategy</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>All regular payments are simple annuities.</td>
        <td>Ask learners to compare the payment interval with the interest-conversion interval.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The annual nominal rate is always the periodic rate.</td>
        <td>Demonstrate division by the number of conversion periods when appropriate.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Deferred means payments are unequal.</td>
        <td>Emphasize that deferred refers to when payments begin, not whether they are equal.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>n is always the number of years.</td>
        <td>Explain that n represents the number of payment periods.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Suggested Remediation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Give learners three blank timelines labeled monthly, quarterly, and annually.</li>
      <li>Provide payment and conversion frequencies separately.</li>
      <li>Have learners draw arrows showing the payment dates.</li>
      <li>Use a two-column classification chart: “Payment Interval” and “Interest-Conversion Interval.”</li>
      <li>Allow calculator-supported practice after learners demonstrate the classification process.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Enrichment</h2>
    <p>Ask advanced learners to investigate a real financial product involving recurring payments and determine whether the payment and interest-conversion intervals coincide. Learners should report the payment frequency, stated interest frequency, and classification with a justification.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h2>Differentiation</h2>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="info">
        <strong>For Learners Needing Support</strong>
        Use visual timelines, frequency tables, guided questions, and partially completed formulas.
      </div>

      <div class="info">
        <strong>For Average Learners</strong>
        Use classification followed by routine calculation and interpretation.
      </div>

      <div class="info">
        <strong>For Learners Ready for Extension</strong>
        Introduce more complex general-annuity situations and ask learners to explain why the simple-annuity formula cannot be applied directly without adjusting the periodic rate appropriately.
      </div>

      <div class="info">
        <strong>Technology Integration</strong>
        Use a scientific calculator or spreadsheet to verify computations after learners establish the correct mathematical model.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

